      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we need to use the standard form of the equation of an ellipse. The general form of the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis and \\(b\\) is the semi-minor axis. The distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\) if the major axis is along the x-axis, or \\(c = \\sqrt{b^2 - a^2}\\) if the major axis is along the y-axis.\n\nFrom the given ellipse, we can see that it has a horizontal major axis and is centered at \\((1,1)\\). The ellipse is defined by the points \\((1,1)\\) and the fact that it is scaled by a factor of \\(2\\) along the x-axis and \\(6\\) along the y-axis. Therefore, we have \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 2\\).\n\nSince the major axis is along the x-axis, we use the formula \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{36 - 4} = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is \\(2c\\):\n\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\times 4\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci is \\(\\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}}\\).",
