      "text": "The given ellipse in the diagram is centered at \\((1,1)\\) with a horizontal major axis. To determine the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes of the ellipse, we need to identify the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes.\n\nThe center of the ellipse is \\((1,1)\\), the right focus is at \\((1 + 2, 1) = (3,1)\\), and the left focus is at \\((1 - 2, 1) = (-1,1)\\). The distance from the center \\((1,1)\\) to either focus is the focal distance, which is 2 units.\n\nFirst, recall the relationship between the semi-major axis \\(a\\), the semi-minor axis \\(b\\), and the distance to the foci \\(c\\):\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\nHere, \\(c = 2\\) and \\(a = 3\\) (since the ellipse has a horizontal major axis and the right focus is at \\((3,1)\\), indicating \\(a = 3\\)). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\n2 = \\sqrt{3^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{9 - b^2}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we obtain:\n\\[\n4 = 9 - b^2 \\implies b^2 = 5 \\implies b = \\sqrt{5}\n\\]\nNow, we can find the distance between the foci. Since the foci are located at \\((3,1)\\) and \\((-1,1)\\), the distance between them is:\n\\[\n|3 - (-1)| = 3 + 1 = 4\n\\]\nThus, the distance between the foci is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
